e  forms that appear  throughout the series obviously parallel  evolution  of the same exact species was virtually  impossible  so the idea of a singular race seeding  genetically similar life throughout the  universe  made a lot of sense this is also similar  to the explanation given  in the star trek universe to explain the  similarities between the inhabitants of  the milky way  star trek began airing in 1966  but it wasn't explained why many alien  life forms and humans are similar  until the second series star trek the  next generation  humans vulcans klingons and many  others share similar characteristics  it is revealed in the 1993 episode of  star trek the next generation  the chase that all humanoid aliens had  originated from a single species  the klingons and the cardassians reject  this knowledge when it's given  insistent on racial superiority while  the romulan captain however  expresses to captain picard that perhaps  the two species are not as different as  they  seem even when aliens don't necessarily  look like humans  alien races and science fiction are  often used as a way for writers to make  analogs for various social  and political groups of people in their  own societies  for instance in star trek the next  generation  while the federation represents a  socialist utopia  the alien life forms known as the  ferengi who were originally intended to  replace the klingons as the main  adversary of the federation  represented the capitalist of old earth  capitalism no longer existed on earth  during this time  due to the fact that scarcity had been  eliminated through technology  the ferengi are repeatedly depicted as  morally bankrupt  and willing to do almost anything to  turn a profit  the depiction of capitalism in star trek  the next generation  is largely due to the creator gene  roddenberry's strong anti-capitalist  views  gene roddenberry considered himself a  humanist he rejected religion from a  young age  and was outspoken about his views